The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions and statements of its President concerning the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in particular resolutions 2717 (2023), 2746 (2024), 2765 (2024) and 2773 (2025), and the press statement of 26 January 2025 on the situation in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and of other States in the region, and emphasizing the need to respect fully the principles of non-interference, good-neighbourliness, regional cooperation and the prohibition of the use of force,

Welcoming the signing on 27 June 2025 in Washington, D.C., of a peace agreement between the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda, following the provisional peace treaty of 19 June 2025, and the commitments contained therein regarding the disengagement, disarmament and conditional integration of armed groups, the return of refugees and respect for territorial integrity,

Welcoming the continued regional and international efforts, including by the African Union, the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community, the Economic Community of Central African States, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, the Luanda and Nairobi processes, and the facilitation of the United States of America, as well as the efforts of the Secretary-General and his Special Envoy for the Great Lakes Region,

Reiterating that there can be no military solution to the situation in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo,

Expressing its deep concern at the continuing deteriorating security and humanitarian situation in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, including the activities of the 23 March Movement (M23) and other armed groups, reports of the presence and support of the Rwanda Defence Forces, the large-scale displacement of civilians, and the serious violations of international humanitarian law and violations and abuses of human rights law, including sexual and gender-based violence and the recruitment and use of children,

Condemning all attacks directed against civilians, humanitarian personnel, medical personnel and facilities, United Nations personnel and peacekeepers, and stressing the need to ensure accountability for all such acts,

Gravely concerned by the continued illicit exploitation of and trafficking in natural resources in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, and underscoring the need to address the root causes of the conflict, including illegal exploitation and the financing of armed groups,

Reaffirming that the Government of the Democratic Republic of the Congo bears the primary responsibility for ensuring security in its territory and protecting its civilians with respect for the rule of law, international human rights law and international humanitarian law,

Reaffirming its full support for the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (MONUSCO) and the importance of ensuring the safety, security and freedom of movement of United Nations and associated personnel,

Determining that the situation in the Democratic Republic of the Congo continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Welcomes the peace agreement signed on 27 June 2025 between the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda, and calls upon both States to implement it fully, without delay and in good faith, including through the disengagement, disarmament and conditional integration of armed groups, the return of refugees, the strict respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and the strengthening of mutual trust and good-neighbourly relations;

2. Demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities and conclude an immediate and unconditional ceasefire, and that the M23 immediately cease all offensive operations, withdraw from Goma, Bukavu and all other occupied areas, including land and lake routes, reverse the establishment of illegitimate parallel administrations, and refrain from any action that impedes the implementation of the peace agreement and the withdrawal of forces;

3. Calls upon the Rwanda Defence Forces to cease support to the M23 and to withdraw immediately from the territory of the Democratic Republic of the Congo without preconditions, consistent with the commitments under the peace agreement and with resolution 2773 (2025), and calls for this withdrawal to be verified through agreed mechanisms;

4. Calls for the urgent implementation of agreed disarmament, demobilization, reintegration and conditional integration programmes for armed groups, with appropriate human rights vetting, and for the implementation of the harmonized plan for the neutralization of the Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda (FDLR) and the disengagement of forces, and in this regard condemns support provided by military forces to specific armed groups, in particular the FDLR, and calls for the cessation of such support;

5. Calls upon the parties to establish, operationalize and strengthen verification and monitoring mechanisms, including the Reinforced Ad-hoc Verification Mechanism, with the support of the African Union, the Secretary-General, MONUSCO and regional mechanisms as appropriate, and requests the Secretary-General to provide technical support to these efforts;

6. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate, in accordance with relevant obligations of international humanitarian law and consistent with humanitarian principles, the full, safe, immediate and unhindered access of humanitarian personnel, equipment and supplies and the timely delivery of humanitarian assistance to populations in need, and calls for the urgent opening of temporary humanitarian corridors in North and South Kivu, the immediate and unrestricted reopening of the airport in Goma, and the continued opening of Kavumu airport and border crossings for the voluntary and safe movement of civilians and humanitarian access;

7. Demands that all parties respect the civilian and humanitarian character of refugee camps and sites for internally displaced persons, protect civilians, including women and children, refrain from all forms of sexual and gender-based violence and from the recruitment or use of children, and ensure the safe and voluntary return of refugees and displaced persons;

8. Demands that all parties respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, ensure accountability for war crimes, crimes against humanity and other serious violations, including summary executions, sexual and gender-based violence, attacks on humanitarian and medical personnel and facilities, and attacks against United Nations and associated personnel, and cooperate fully with United Nations mechanisms;

9. Reaffirms its full support for MONUSCO in the implementation of its mandate, in particular the protection of civilians, and demands that all parties immediately cease all hostilities against peacekeepers, take all appropriate measures to ensure the safety and security of United Nations personnel, guarantee their freedom of movement and the unimpeded implementation of their mandate, including the unimpeded return of MONUSCO personnel to Goma; stresses that attacks against peacekeepers may constitute war crimes and that planning, directing, sponsoring or participating in such attacks constitutes a basis for sanctions designations; and demands the cessation of GPS jamming and spoofing and of the deployment of surface-to-air missiles and any other threat to the air operations of MONUSCO, regional missions and humanitarian actors;

10. Condemns the systematic illicit exploitation and trafficking of natural resources in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, calls for renewed efforts to ensure transparency and traceability of mineral exports, and urges all companies operating in the mineral sector to exercise due diligence in line with relevant Security Council resolutions and the Regional Certification Mechanism of the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region;

11. Reaffirms the primary responsibility of the Government of the Democratic Republic of the Congo to reinforce State authority and governance in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, including through the rule of law, security sector reform and socio-economic development, and calls upon international partners to support these efforts;

12. Reaffirms its support for the Luanda and Nairobi processes and for enhanced coordination, harmonization and complementarity among African Union, East African Community, Southern African Development Community and International Conference on the Great Lakes Region efforts, calls for the resumption and intensification of political consultations under the Nairobi Process to address the issue of armed groups, including the M23, and requests the Secretary-General, through the Office of the Special Envoy for the Great Lakes Region and in collaboration with MONUSCO, to continue supporting these regional peace efforts;

13. Emphasizes its readiness to consider additional measures, in accordance with resolution 2293 (2016), including against those who contribute to the continuation of the conflict in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, and calls upon all Member States to implement fully the existing sanctions measures;

14. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution and of the peace agreement, including on the ceasefire, withdrawals, disarmament and demobilization, humanitarian access, protection of civilians and the support provided by the United Nations system, and to provide recommendations on how the Council can best support the implementation of the agreement;

15. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

