1. Welcomes the peace agreement signed on 27 June 2025 between the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Rwanda, and calls upon both States to implement it fully, without delay and in good faith, under African ownership and in close coordination with the African Union and complementary to the Luanda and Nairobi processes, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations and relevant Security Council resolutions, and in full respect of the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and of all States in the region, including through the disengagement, disarmament and conditional integration of armed groups, the return of refugees, strict respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and of all States in the region, and the strengthening of mutual trust and good-neighbourly relations; and emphasizes the importance of the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women in the implementation of the agreement and in peacebuilding efforts;

2. Demands that all parties immediately cease hostilities and conclude an immediate and unconditional ceasefire, and that the M23 immediately cease all offensive operations, withdraw from Goma, Bukavu and all other occupied areas, including land and lake routes, reverse the establishment of illegitimate parallel administrations, and refrain from any action that impedes the implementation of the peace agreement and the withdrawal of forces;

3. Demands that the Rwanda Defence Forces cease all support to the M23, including direct and indirect support, and withdraw immediately and unconditionally from the territory of the Democratic Republic of the Congo without preconditions, consistent with the commitments under the peace agreement and with resolution 2773 (2025), as part of the withdrawal of all foreign forces from the territory of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and calls for this withdrawal to be verified through agreed mechanisms, including with MONUSCO support as appropriate;

4. Calls for the urgent implementation of agreed disarmament, demobilization, reintegration and conditional integration programmes for armed groups, with appropriate human rights vetting, including screening for grave violations against children and conflict-related sexual and gender-based violence, and in accordance with applicable national legislation and international obligations, excluding individuals responsible for war crimes, crimes against humanity or other serious violations, and for the implementation of the harmonized plan for the neutralization of the Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda (FDLR) and the disengagement of forces, and in this regard condemns support provided by military forces to specific armed groups, in particular the FDLR, and calls for the cessation of such support;

5. Calls upon the parties to establish, operationalize and strengthen verification and monitoring mechanisms, including the Reinforced Ad-hoc Verification Mechanism, under the leadership of the African Union, in close coordination with the Secretary-General, MONUSCO, and regional mechanisms including the East African Community, the Southern African Development Community and the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region as appropriate, and with the consent of the Government of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, without prejudice to MONUSCO's mandate, and requests the Secretary-General to provide technical support to these efforts;

6. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate, in accordance with relevant obligations of international humanitarian law and consistent with humanitarian principles, the full, safe, immediate and unhindered access of humanitarian personnel, equipment and supplies and the timely delivery of humanitarian assistance to populations in need, and calls for the urgent opening of temporary humanitarian corridors in North and South Kivu, the immediate and unrestricted reopening of the airport in Goma, and the continued opening of Kavumu airport and border crossings for the voluntary and safe movement of civilians and humanitarian access;

7. Demands that all parties respect the civilian and humanitarian character of refugee camps and sites for internally displaced persons, protect civilians, including women and children, refrain from all forms of sexual and gender-based violence and from the recruitment or use of children, and ensure the safe and voluntary return of refugees and displaced persons;

8. Demands that all parties respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, ensure accountability for war crimes, crimes against humanity and other serious violations, including summary executions, sexual and gender-based violence, attacks on humanitarian and medical personnel and facilities, and attacks against United Nations and associated personnel, and cooperate fully with United Nations mechanisms; and calls upon all parties to cooperate with relevant accountability mechanisms, including, as appropriate, national judicial mechanisms and other relevant international and regional mechanisms, consistent with international law;

9. Reaffirms its full support for MONUSCO in the implementation of its mandate, in particular the protection of civilians, and demands that all parties immediately cease all hostilities against peacekeepers, take all appropriate measures to ensure the safety and security of United Nations personnel, guarantee their freedom of movement and the unimpeded implementation of their mandate, including the unimpeded return of MONUSCO personnel to Goma; stresses that attacks against peacekeepers may constitute war crimes and that planning, directing, sponsoring or participating in such attacks constitutes a basis for sanctions designations; and demands the cessation of GPS jamming and spoofing and of the deployment of surface-to-air missiles and any other threat to the air operations of MONUSCO, regional missions and humanitarian actors;

10. Condemns the systematic illicit exploitation and trafficking of natural resources in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, calls for renewed efforts to ensure transparency and traceability of mineral exports, and urges all companies operating in the mineral sector to exercise due diligence in line with relevant Security Council resolutions and the Regional Certification Mechanism of the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region, and calls for strengthened international and regional cooperation, including through the tracking of financial resources and illicit financial flows, to disrupt the financing of armed groups in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo;

11. Reaffirms the primary responsibility of the Government of the Democratic Republic of the Congo to reinforce State authority and governance in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, including through the rule of law, security sector reform and socio-economic development, and calls upon international partners to support these efforts;

12. Calls upon the international community to scale up humanitarian and development assistance to the Democratic Republic of the Congo and the region, in accordance with humanitarian principles and national priorities, and to support the Government of the Democratic Republic of the Congo in strengthening State authority and socio-economic development;

13. Reaffirms its support for the Luanda and Nairobi processes, including their merged and complementary implementation, and for enhanced coordination, harmonization and complementarity among African Union, East African Community, Southern African Development Community and International Conference on the Great Lakes Region efforts, in line with the principle of African ownership; calls for the resumption and intensification of political consultations under the Nairobi Process, with the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women, youth and civil society, to address the issue of armed groups, including the M23; welcomes the African Union Peace and Security Council's call for a Quadripartite meeting of the East African Community, the Economic Community of Central African States, the International Conference on the Great Lakes Region and the Southern African Development Community and calls for its urgent convening; underlines that the implementation and monitoring of the peace agreement should be led by the parties with the support of the African Union and regional mechanisms, in coordination with the United Nations; and requests the Secretary-General, through the Office of the Special Envoy for the Great Lakes Region and in collaboration with MONUSCO, to continue supporting these regional peace efforts;

14. Reaffirms its intention to consider targeted sanctions, in accordance with resolution 2293 (2016), against those who contribute to the continuation of the conflict in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo, if necessary and in close consultation with the African Union and regional countries, and on the basis of the Secretary-General's report, and in full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Democratic Republic of the Congo; requests the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1533 (2004) to consider pending designations without undue delay, in accordance with its established procedures and in close coordination with the peace process; and calls upon all Member States to implement fully the existing sanctions measures;

15. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 60 days on the implementation of this resolution and of the peace agreement, including on the ceasefire, withdrawals, including the withdrawal of Rwanda Defence Forces and the functioning of agreed verification mechanisms, disarmament and demobilization, humanitarian access, protection of civilians, including on the implementation of the women, peace and security agenda, child protection and sexual and gender-based violence, and the support provided by the United Nations system, and to provide recommendations on how the Council can best support the implementation of the agreement;

16. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.
